## The query

The fast way to get "top N per group" in SQL Server is a correlated subquery (`CROSS/OUTER APPLY`), not `GROUP BY` + window functions re-scanning the whole table. In EF Core 8 this comes out of a per-customer `Take(3)` over the navigation collection, which the SQL Server provider translates into exactly that `APPLY` shape:

```csharp
// src/Api/Controllers/OrdersController.cs
[HttpGet("recent")]
public async Task<IActionResult> GetRecentPerCustomer([FromQuery] int page = 0, [FromQuery] int pageSize = 100)
{
    var result = await db.Customers
        .OrderBy(c => c.Id)
        .Skip(page * pageSize)
        .Take(pageSize)
        .Select(c => new
        {
            c.Id,
            c.Name,
            RecentOrders = c.Orders
                .OrderByDescending(o => o.PlacedAt)
                .Take(3)
                .Select(o => new { o.Id, o.Total, o.PlacedAt })
        })
        .ToListAsync();

    return Ok(result);
}

